Y28 $210M; FY29 $225M; FY30 $230M [Assumption].\n\u0394WORKING CAPITAL: FY26 \u2212$120M (inventory build to buffer supply chain [Actual call]); FY27-30 \u2212$45 to \u2212$60M/yr [Assumption].\nSBC: $35M\u2192$47M path [Assumption \u2014 ~0.7-0.8% of revenue, consistent with history [Actual]].\nDISCOUNT RATE (WACC): Rf = 4.70% [Actual]; ERP = 4.75% [Assumption]; beta = 1.0 (pack gives 0.881 [Estimate], but cyclicality argues for 1.0 [Assumption]); CoE = 4.70 + 1.0\u00d74.75 = 9.45%. CoD = 6.0% pre-tax [Assumption \u2014 investment grade ~BBB spread 1.3% over 10Y; rating [Unconfirmed]]. Debt weight ~1.8% (net debt $375M vs equity $20.3B). WACC \u2248 9.4%; rounded to 9.5% for cyclical conservatism.\nTERMINAL GROWTH: 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 below long-run nominal GDP ~3.5-4%, consistent with a cyclical that cannot outgrow GDP in perpetuity].\n\nENGINE 1 \u2014 QUALITY DCF (secondary anchor; 40% weight)\n| FY | Rev ($B) | NI Margin | NI ($M) | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC | SBC | FCF (OE) |\n|----|----------|-----------|---------|-----|-------|-----|-----|----------|\n| 26E| 4.350 | 13.1% | 570 | 165 | 290 | \u2212120 | 35 | 290 |\n| 27E| 4.742 | 13.4% | 635 | 195 | 240 | \u221255 | 38 | 497 |\n| 28E| 5.073 | 13.8% | 700 | 210 | 220 | \u221260 | 41 | 589 |\n| 29E| 5.327 | 14.0% | 746 | 225 | 205 | \u221250 | 44 | 672 |\n| 30E| 5.540 | 14.0% | 776 | 230 | 210 | \u221245 | 47 | 704 |\nPV of FCFs @9.5%: 265+415+449+467+447 = $2,043M\nTerminal FCF = 704\u00d71.03 = $725M; TV = 725/(0.095\u22120.03) = $11,154M; PV(TV) = $7,086M\nEV = $9,129M; less net debt $375M (debt $702M [10-K] \u2212 cash $327M [Filed]) = equity $8,754M\nDCF PER SHARE = 8,754 / 59.203 = $148 [Estimate]\nSensitivity (tornado, \u00b11\u03c3):\n\u2022 WACC 8.5% vs 10.5% \u2192 $177 vs $121 (biggest lever)\n\u2022 FY30 margin 13% vs 15% \u2192 $135 vs $162\n\u2022 Long-run growth 4% vs 2% \u2192 $166 vs $132\nTop-3 variables = WACC, cycle duration (FY28-29 revenue), terminal/capex normalization.\nENGINE 2 \u2014 CYCLE-ADJUSTED (primary; 60% weight):\n\u2022 Peak (FY26E): EPS $9.40 [Arithmetic/Actual-guided], net margin 13.1%\n\u2022 Mid-cycle: normalized net margin 11-12% on mid-cycle revenue ~$3.7-3.9B [Assumption \u2014 above FY19-23 experience of 7-9% margin because mix improved structurally] \u2192 mid-cycle EPS \u2248 $6.80-7.90 [Estimate]\n\u2022 Normal mid-cycle multiple for a quality cycle: 15-18x [Assumption]\n\u2022 Cycle-adjusted value: $102-142; call it $125 [Estimate]\n\u2022 Bear-trough (FY29 recession): EPS $6.0-6.5 [Assumption], 12-14x \u2192 $75-95\nENGINE 4 \u2014 REVERSE DCF (expectation audit):\nAt $343, EV = $20.7B. With WACC 9.5% and g 3.0%, required FCF1 = EV\u00d7(WACC\u2212g) = $1.35B \u2014 vs actual $290-325M. Even with a 5-year 15% FCF ramp, the model requires 8+ years of 15% FCF growth or ~12 years of 10% to justify $343.\nBLENDED INTRINSIC VALUE (Base): 0.4\u00d7$148 + 0.6\u00d7$125 = $134 (mechanical blend). However, the probability-weighted scenario method (Section 4) yields $178; I take that as the operative IV because it explicitly captures the bull case tail. IV BASE = $178 [Estimate]; 50% CI $140-240; 80% CI $110-300.\nVALUATION CROSS-CHECKS vs price $343: P/E trailing 40.3x [Actual]; FY26E 36.5x [Arithmetic]; EV/EBITDA ~23.3x [Arithmetic]; P/FCF ~59x after SBC [Arithmetic]; PEG 2.3 [Actual]; EV/Sales 4.9x [Arithmetic]. All are at or ABOVE the historical premium band for an aerospace cyclical (HEI/TDG are the only clean comparables at similar multiples, and they have materially higher aftermarket margin content; CW/MOG trade 20-30x \u2014 [Unconfirmed current peer multiples]).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 Competitive response: if WWD's 10% pricing holds, engine OEMs (CFM, P&W) will push harder on cost-down clauses at next LTA renewal; risk of make-vs-buy on actuation content: \u22121 (modest).\n\u2022 Reflexivity: HIGH STOCK PRICE \u2192 SBC is cheap currency, M&A optionality (\"flexibility for strategic opportunities\" [Actual call]); LOW STOCK PRICE \u2192 minimal damage (no equity funding need; credit is investment-grade). Positive reflexivity is currently fading as price falls.\n\u2022 Customer evolution: installed base of LEAP/GTF is young; aftermarket spares revenue compounds for 20+ years. LTV/CAC/churn [Unconfirmed] \u2014 but utilization-driven services should keep growing even if OE dips.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPRIOR (Layer 1.5 base rates): 30-40% probability of a 40%+ drawdown within 3 years for a cyclical at peak.\nUPDATE EVIDENCE (Q3 FY26 [Actual]): +21% revenue, +43% adj EPS, guidance raised, pricing +10%, backlog $3.42B. This evidence RAISES the near-term path but does NOT change the cyclical character.\nPOSTERIOR SCENARIOS:\n\u2022 BEAR (25%): Aero rate cuts begin FY28 (recession or 737/neo production stalls \u2014 both already at 42-50/mo, near historical peaks); China wind-down \u22124% Industrial; data-center power capex pauses. Peak EPS $10.40 FY27, then $7.00-8.00. Intrinsic: $110. 12-mo price: $240 (28x on $8.50 trough-ish EPS [Assumption]).\n\u2022 BASE (45%): Upcycle persists through FY29; aftermarket +10-12%; industrial ex-China +8-10%; margins hold 13.5-14.5%; no recession before FY30. Intrinsic: $160. 12-mo price: $295 [Estimate] (28.4x on FY27E EPS $10.40 [Assumption]).\n\u2022 BULL (30%): Re-rating to quality compounder; pricing +5% persists; data-center + defense surprise up; EPS reaches $14-15 by FY30; exit multiple 25x. Intrinsic: $260. 12-mo price: $380 (33x on FY27E EPS $11.50 [Assumption]).\nEXPECTED INTRINSIC = 0.25\u00d7110 + 0.45\u00d7160 + 0.30\u00d7260 = $178 [Estimate]\nEXPECTED 12-MONTH PRICE = 0.25\u00d7240 + 0.45\u00d7295 + 0.30\u00d7380 = $307 [Estimate]\nMARGIN OF SAFETY = (178 \u2212 343.26)/178 = \u221293% \u2192 NONE (deeply negative).\nNote: I checked prob my 25/45/30 weights; base is highest single \u2014 consistent.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u255