50\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rates:\n\u2022 5-year survival for a 155-year-old, investment-grade, $3.42B-backlog capital-goods incumbent: >95% (base rate high) [Estimate]\n\u2022 Cyclical peak \u2192 40%+ drawdown within 3 years: 30\u201340% (general industrials base rate) [Estimate]\n\u2022 Aero supplier aftermarket/service annuity success: HIGH base rate \u2014 WWD services already 35%+ of aero revenue at +24% growth (this is an existing feature, not a bet) [Estimate]\n\u2022 Large M&A (>$10B): N/A \u2014 WWD does small tuck-ins only. [Actual]\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate: My central scenario assumes NO severe downcycle through FY2030. Justification: (1) backlog +18% to $3.42B, 78% executable within 12 months [Estimate from FY25 release via Panabee]; (2) demonstrated 10% pricing realization in Q3 FY26 [Actual company call] \u2014 suggests scarcity, not commodity; (3) after-market driven by record LEAP/GTF installed base + high fleet utilization, which is counter-cyclical. These justify a milder bear than the historical base rate but do NOT justify eliminating the bear case.\nStep 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check: My bull case (30% probability of sustained 15% EPS growth) sits at ~1.3-1.5\u00d7 of what base rates for cyclicals would suggest \u2192 requires additional evidence, but below the 2.0\u00d7 red flag. The MARKET at $343, however, is pricing a success probability closer to 60-70% with no recession \u2014 that is >2\u00d7 base rate. That is the red flag, and it is on the market's side of the ledger.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business: WWD engineers and manufactures fuel-delivery, combustion-control, actuation and electronics for aircraft engines and for industrial power-generation, marine, oil & gas and transport applications. It is a first-tier certified supplier to CFM (LEAP), P&W (GTF), GE, Rolls-Royce (OEM + aftermarket spares) and to the US DoD. Revenue is roughly 62% Aerospace / 38% Industrial [Estimate from filial segment mix]. Understandability: HIGH for a specialist.\nStep 2-2. Moat Score (0-21):\n\u2022 Intangible assets (certifications/engineering know-how): 3\n\u2022 Switching costs (engine-certified, decades-qualified components): 3\n\u2022 Network effects: 0\n\u2022 Cost advantage (scale in niches): 2\n\u2022 Economies of scale: 2\n\u2022 Regulatory barriers (FAA/EASA cert): 3\n\u2022 Data moat (engine-performance databases): 1\nTOTAL = 14/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT. Direction: WIDENING (pricing +10%, aftermarket share growth, capacity additions; watch competitor entry in actuation and engine-OEM vertical integration).\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification: CYCLICAL (with fast-grower traits in this upcycle). Checkpoint: buy on trough \u00d7 low multiple, sell on peak \u00d7 high multiple. WWD currently trades on PEAK earnings at a PREMIUM multiple \u2014 classic Lynch sell zone.\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard:\n\u2022 Revenue growth: FY25 +7.3% [Actual]; TTM to Jun-26 $4.192B, running +21-22% guided FY26 [Actual]\n\u2022 Net margin: FY25 12.4%, TTM 13.2% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 ROE: vendor 22.3% [Actual/Estimate]; FY25 on ending equity 17.2% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 FCF margin: TTM 9.0% (FCF $377M on $4.192B); FY25 9.5% [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 Net debt/EBITDA: ~0.4x my calc (debt \u2248$702M [10-K], cash $327M, EBITDA \u2248 $866M) vs company-stated 1.6x leverage at Jun-26 [Actual call] \u2014 either basis different or leases/pension included; investment grade either way.\n\u2022 Interest coverage \u2248 16x OI/interest [Arithmetic]\nVERDICT: EXCELLENT.\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0-15):\n\u2022 Buyback efficiency: 2/3 ($553M repurchased in 9M FY26 [Actual], but much executed at 30-40x P/E \u2014 timing aggressive, not efficient)\n\u2022 M&A track record: 2/3 (small tuck-ins; Canada electronics acquisition already being closed \u2014 indicates imperfect diligence but quick discipline)\n\u2022 Capex efficiency: 2/3 (incremental ROIC ~19-20% historically [Estimate]; the $290M FY26 step-up is unproven)\n\u2022 Mix rationality: 3/3 (organic > M&A > buybacks; dividend token)\n\u2022 Guidance accuracy: 3/3 (FY26 raised twice; beat in Q1/Q2/Q3)\nTOTAL: 12/15. Insider ownership 0.25% [Actual] \u2192 no bonus; recent insider activity [Unconfirmed]. Net 12/15.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D: WWD's R&D is modest (~3-4% of sales [Unconfirmed]); most development is customer-funded. Capitalizing it adds <1%p to ROIC \u2192 adjustment immaterial.\nStep 2.5-2. SBC: $32M FY25, $33M FY24 [Actual]. Treated as a full expense in my owner earnings. TTM FCF $377M \u2212 SBC ~$32M = $345M adjusted FCF [Arithmetic].\nStep 2.5-3. Operating leases: $26M [Actual] \u2014 immaterial.\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data asset: modest; engineering reputation is valuable but not separable.\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: FY25 NOPAT \u2248 OI $634M [Estimate based on 17.8% vendor OM] \u00d7 (1\u221224%) \u2248 $482M; invested capital = equity $2.566B + debt $0.702B \u2212 cash $0.327B = $2.941B [Arithmetic] \u2192 ROIC \u2248 16.4% [Estimate]; TTM ROIC \u2248 19.0% [Estimate]. GAAP-to-adjusted gap not material (<5%p).\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (FY2025, Buffett): NI $442M + D&A $113M \u2212 maintenance capex ~$90M [Assumption] \u2212 \u0394WC $157M [Arithmetic] = $308M [Arithmetic/Assumption]. This equals reported FCF $340M \u2212 SBC $32M \u2248 $308M \u2014 internally consistent.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (BLENDED ENGINE 2 \u00d7 60% + ENGINE 1 \u00d7 40%)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nFORECAST SOURCES \u2014 every driver is sourced:\nREVENUE: FY26 +22% \u2192 $4.35B (guided +20-23% [Actual]; Q1-Q3 actuals +23%/21%/21% [Actual]). FY27 +9% \u2192 $4.74B (consensus fwd growth 8.95% [Estimate, yfinance]; organic ex-China-wind-down ~13-14% [Assumption]). FY28 +7%; FY29 +5%; FY30 +4% [all Assumption \u2014 airframer ramp 42\u219250-75 MAX/neo [Estimate from SimpleFlying/AirCurrent] but decelerating; after-market plateau]. A decade of runway beyond FY30 is NOT established \u2014 airframes are at the top of historical rate bands.\nNET MARGIN: FY26 13.1% (implied $570M NI; consistent with adjusted EPS $9.30-9.50 guidance [Actual] after GAAP/adj gap); FY27 13.4%; FY28 13.8%; FY29 14.0%; FY30 14.0% [Assumption]. My margins are BELOW Q3 FY26 annualized (segment 24%/22.1% [Actual]) to account for China on-highway wind-down, one-time pricing, and cost inflation \u2014 conservative.\nCAPEX: FY26 $290M [Actual guidance]; FY27 $240M; FY28 $220M; FY29 $205M; FY30 $210M [Assumption \u2014 peak investment in FY26 then normalize to ~4% of sales].\nD&A: FY26 $165M [Estimate \u2014 FY25 $113M [Actual] + new capacity]; FY27 $195M; FY28 $210M; FY29 $225M; FY30 $230M [Assumption].\n\u0394WORKING CAPITAL: FY26 \u2212$120M (inventory build to buffer supply chain [Actual call]); FY27-30 \u2212$45 to \u2212$60M/yr [Assumption].\nSBC: $35M\u2192$47M path [Assumption \u2014 ~0.7-0.8% of revenue, consistent with history [Actual]].\nDISCOUNT RATE (WACC): Rf = 4.70% [Actual]; ERP = 4.75% [Assumption]; beta = 1.0 (pack gives 0.881 [Estimate], but cyclicality argues for 1.0 [Assumption]); CoE = 4.70 + 1.0\u00d74.75 = 9.45%. CoD = 6.0% pre-tax [Assumption \u2014 investment grade ~BBB spread 1.3% over 10Y; rating [Unconfirmed]]. Debt weight ~1.8% (net debt $375M vs equity $20.3B). WACC \u2248 9.4%; rounded to 9.5% for cyclical conservatism.\nTERMINAL GROWTH: 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 below long-run nominal GDP ~3.5-4%, consistent with a cyclical that cannot outgrow GDP in perpetuity].\n\nENGINE 1 \u2014 QUALITY DCF (secondary anchor; 40% weight)\n| FY | Rev ($B) | NI Margin | NI ($M) | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC | SBC | FCF (OE) |\n|----|----------|-----------|---------|-----|-------|-----|-----|----------|\n| 26E| 4.350 | 13.1% | 570 | 165 | 290 | \u2212120 | 35 | 290 |\n| 27E| 4.742 | 13.4% | 635 | 195 | 240 | \u221255 | 38 | 497 |\n| 28E| 5.073 | 13.8% | 700 | 210 | 220 | \u221260 | 41 | 589 |\n| 29E| 5.327 | 14.0% | 746 | 225 | 205 | \u221250 | 44 | 672 |\n| 30E| 5.540 | 14.0% | 776 | 230 | 210 | \u221245 | 47 | 704 |\nPV of FCFs @9.5%: 265+415+449+467+447 = $2,043M\nTerminal FCF = 704\u00d71.03 = $725M; TV = 725/(0.095\u22120.03) = $11,154M; PV(TV) = $7,086M\nEV = $9,129M; less net debt $375M (debt $702M [10-K] \u2212 cash $327M [Filed]) = equity $8,754M\nDCF PER SHARE = 8,754 / 59.203 = $148 [Estimate]\nSensitivity (tornado, \u00b11\u03c3):\n\u2022 WACC 8.5% vs 10.5% \u2192 $177 vs $121 (biggest lever)\n\u2022 FY30 margin 13% vs 15% \u2192 $135 vs $162\n\u2022 Long-run growth 4% vs 2% \u2192 $166 vs $132\nTop-3 variables = WACC, cycle duration (FY28-29 revenue), terminal/capex normalization.\nENGINE 2 \u2014 CYCLE-ADJUSTED (primary; 60% weight):\n\u2022 Peak (FY26E): EPS $9.40 [Arithmetic/Actual-guided], net margin 13.1%\n\u2022 Mid-cycle: normalized net margin 11-12% on mid-cycle revenue ~$3.7-3.9B [Assumption \u2014 above FY19-23 experience of 7-9% margin because mix improved structurally] \u2192 mid-cycle EPS \u2248 $6.80-7.90 [Estimate]\n\u2022 Normal mid-cycle multiple for a quality cycle: 15-18x [Assumption]\n\u2022 Cycle-adjusted value: $102-142; call it $125 [Estimate]\n\u2022 Bear-trough (FY29 recession): EPS $6.0-6.5 [Assumption], 12-14x \u2192 $75-95\nENGINE 4 \u2014 REVERSE DCF (expectation audit):\nAt $343, EV = $20.7B. With WACC 9.5% and g 3.0%, required FCF1 = EV\u00d7(WACC\u2212g) = $1.35B \u2014 vs actual $290-325M. Even with a 5-year 15% FCF ramp, the model requires 8+ years of 15% FCF growth or ~12 years of 10% to justify $343.\nBLENDED INTRINSIC VALUE (Base): 0.4\u00d7$148 + 0.6\u00d7$125 = $134 (mechanical blend). However, the probability-weighted scenario method (Section 4) yields $178; I take that as the operative IV because it explicitly captures the bull case tail. IV BASE = $178 [Estimate]; 50% CI $140-240; 80% CI $110-300.\nVALUATION CROSS-CHECKS vs price $343: P/E trailing 40.3x [Actual]; FY26E 36.5x [Arithmetic]; EV/EBITDA ~23.3x [Arithmetic]; P/FCF ~59x after SBC [Arithmetic]; PEG 2.3 [Actual]; EV/Sales 4.9x [Arithmetic]. All are at or ABOVE the historical premium band for an aerospace cyclical (HEI/TDG are the only clean comparables at similar multiples, and they have materially higher aftermarket margin content; CW/MOG trade 20-30x \u2014 [Unconfirmed current peer multiples]).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u