36.5x FY26E adj.\n- Short % float: 2.23 (\u221227.6% MoM) | Institutional: 93.8% | Insider: 0.25%\n\n**Key data-quality notes (applies to whole report):**\n1. The pack's section continuity warning was checked. No unexplained one-way step discontinuities found in WWD's filed series; Capex agrees with vendor; TTM figures reconcile with filed quarters.\n2. Segment detail is not in the pack (\"WE HAVE NONE\"). I sourced segment data from Woodward's own Q3 FY26 press release and 10-K [Actual].\n3. R&D expense is not in the pack and could not be retrieved within the research budget \u2192 **[Unconfirmed]**, carried as an explicit [Assumption] in Section 2.5.\n4. FY27/28 analyst estimates, DXY, credit spreads, peer forward multiples, and the earnings-call Q&A could not be retrieved \u2192 **[Unconfirmed]** where used, carried into scenario dispersion.\n\n**Headline conclusion:** Woodward is a high-quality, cyclically-exposed controls business executing superbly. At $343.26, however, the market capitalises ~$20.3B \u2014 36.5x forward adjusted EPS and >60x guided free cash flow \u2014 implying a decade of uninterrupted double-digit growth. That is 1.8\u20132.5x a disciplined cycle-adjusted intrinsic value. **No margin of safety exists.** Recommendation: **Avoid at T0; do not initiate. Re-evaluate at $235\u2013$255.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1 \u2014 MACRO CONTEXT\n\n### Step 1-1. Macro Variables\n| Variable | Level | Direction | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10-Yr Treasury | 4.65\u20134.71% | Slightly up (+0.04pt MoM; +0.44pt YoY) | [Retrieved \u2014 YCharts/TradingEconomics, Aug 2026] |\n| Real rates | ~2.0\u20132.2% (4.7% \u2212 ~2.5% breakeven) | [Estimate] | Fed H.15 TIPS implied |\n| Fed Funds | 3.50\u20133.75% | On hold 5th straight meeting; ~1/3 market odds of a hike | [Retrieved \u2014 TradingEconomics/FOMC, Jul 2026] |\n| DXY | **[Unconfirmed] \u2014 not retrieved** | Elevated gold ($4,698) suggests USD-gold tension, stagflationary | [Estimate] |\n| Global liquidity | Tightening end-phase; central banks cautious | Sticky | [Estimate] |\n| Cycle position | Late expansion, rising recession risk | \u2014 | [Estimate] |\n| Inflation | Sticky, above target; gold at quasi-record | Persistent | [Retrieved \u2014 market data] |\n| Credit spreads | **[Unconfirmed] \u2014 not retrieved** | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n\n### Step 1-2. Regime Probability Distribution\n| Regime | Probability | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Goldilocks (low rates + growth) | 15% | Rates are NOT low (10Y ~4.7%) |\n| Reflation (high rates + growth) | 30% | Fed hold + positive aero/industrial momentum |\n| **Stagflation (high rates + low growth)** | **35%** | Gold ~$4,700, hike odds 1/3, sticky inflation, late-cycle |\n| Recession (low rates + low growth) | 20% | Fed hiking odds, yield curve pressure, late cycle |\n| **Total** | **100%** | \u2014 |\n\n### Step 1-3. Sensitivity Matrix (per \u00b1 1 regime shift)\n| Axis | Score (\u22123 to +3) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interest rate sensitivity | \u22122 | Capital-intensive customer demand (aero capex, grid capex) discouraged by high rates; WACC directly affected |\n| Dollar sensitivity | 0 | Global sales, US-based costs; natural hedge |\n| Liquidity sensitivity | +1 | Defense + aftermarket are sticky; long-cycle programs funded |\n| Economic cycle sensitivity | \u22122 | Aerospace OEM rate changes with airline profitability; industrial demand cyclical |\n| Commodity sensitivity | \u22121 | Castings/forgings/rare-earth input costs; inflation pressure |\n\n### Step 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment\nRegime weights \u00d7 sensitivities \u2192 **weighted macro impact: \u22120.6\u03c3** \u2014 **Mild Headwind**. In stagflation/recession WWD's earnings compress; in reflation it thrives. Net-net: High rates + sticky inflation + late-cycle = headwind to multiple and to near-term FCF conversion. **Conviction \u22120.5 steps. Strengthen entry discipline.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1.5 \u2014 BASE RATE CHECK\n\n### Step 1.5-1. Base rates (framework reference)\n- Cycical industrial supplier 5-year survival: ~90%+\n- Cyclical peak \u2192 40%+ drawdown within 3 years: **30\u201340%**\n- \"Aerospace supercycle\" narratives achieving 10%+ sustained growth for 5+ years: **~25\u201335%**\n- Downturn EPS peak-to-trough decline in aero-supply chains (2020 example): **\u221230% to \u221250%**\n- Large buyback at elevated multiple (leverage-funded) ending poorly: elevated base rate (no formal statistic; see Red Team)\n\n### Step 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate\nMy base case assumes WWD can grow 7\u20139% through FY28 and sustain 18.5% EBIT margin. The base rate for cyclical aero suppliers suggests the current upcycle will roll over within 3 years. Company-specific justifications for deviation:\n1. Commercial aero OEM production rates are still ~50\u201360% of planned recovery; Boeing 737 & Airbus A320 targets imply 5+ years of rate increases. [Actual/Estimate \u2014 industry commentary retrieved]\n2. Aftermarket (services = ~30% of Aero revenue, growing 20\u201324% in FY26) is structurally growing with fleet age; licenses for LEAP repairs extend content. [Actual \u2014 Q3 FY26 release, segment detail]\n3. Industrial data-center/grid power demand has 3+ year visibility; management cites customers raising forecasts into early 2030s. [Actual/Estimate \u2014 Zacks/Yahoo Aug/2026]\n\nDeviation is justified, but not to the degree the current price implies (see Layer 3).\n\n### Step 1.5-3. Shock Check\nMy bull case assumes ~30% probability of sustained double-digit growth. Base rate for the same class is ~25\u201335%. Inside 1.5x band \u2014 no red flag on its own. But the market price implies **>65%** probability of sustained double-digit growth with durable 20%+ margins. **This breaches 2.0x \u2014 Red Flag: the current price embeds a base-rate violation.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n### Step 2-1. Business Understanding\nWoodward designs and manufactures energy-conversion and control systems (fuel controls, actuators, combustion systems, electrical power) for two markets: Aerospace (commercial OEM, commercial services/aftermarket, defense OEM, defense services) and Industrial (transportation, power generation, oil & gas). It is the global leader in several engine-platform niches, with products that require years of certification and typically last the life of the engine. Understandability: **High** on the business model; **Medium** on the cycle timing.\n\n### Step 2-2. Moat Assessment\n| Moat type | Score (0\u20133) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (certifications, IP, FAA/EASA approvals) | 3 | Widening (LEAP repair licenses, electric-propulsion IP) |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Widening (cradle-to-grave engine controls, legacy OEM positions) |\n| Network effects | 0 | N/A |\n| Cost advantage | 1 | Stable (scale, niche manufacturing; not lowest-cost in broad markets) |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening (capacity expansion Glatten +50%, Spartanburg 2027) |\n| Regulatory barriers | 2 | Stable (aviation certification barriers) |\n| Data moat | 1 | Stable (fleet operating data, service analytics) |\n| **Total** | **12/21 \u2014 Wide Moat** | **Stable-to-Widening** |\n\n### Step 2-3. Lynch Classification\n**Cyclical with Stalwart tendencies** \u2014 Commercial OEM rate cycle is the primary earnings driver; aftermarket and defense provide a floor; industrial is a portfolio of mini-cycles. Key checkpoints: production-rate trajectory, services-to-OEM mix, inventory health.\n\n### Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR (5-yr, FY20\u2013FY25) | +7.4% | [Actual \u2014 Arithmetic on pack] |\n| Revenue TTM growth | +21\u201324% | [Actual \u2014 10-Q/press release] |\n| Operating margin FY25 / TTM | 12.4% / 17.8% | [Actual/Arithmetic] |\n| GAAP ROE | 22.3% | [Actual \u2014 vendor] |\n| ROIC GAAP (FY25) | ~17.2% | [Estimate \u2014 NOPAT/invested capital] |\n| FCF margin TTM | 9.0% | [Actual \u2014 pack] |\n| Net debt/EBITDA | 1.6x (Q3 FY26) | [Actual \u2014 company release] |\n| Piotroski F-score | 8/8 evaluated | [Actual \u2014 Arithmetic] |\n| Accruals ratio | \u22120.0065 (low accrual) | [Actual \u2014 Arithmetic] |\n\n**Judgment: Excellent** financial quality. The sole concerns are FCF-lagging-EPS (capex + working capital) and debt-funded buybacks.\n\n### Step 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard\n| Item | Score (0\u20133) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 0 | $553M buybacks YTD FY26 at >30x forward EPS, funded partly by debt (total debt $1.34B, 1.6x leverage, vs $0.58B LY) |\n| M&A track record | 2 | No major impairments visible; tuck-in history; not quantified from pack |\n| Capex efficiency | 2 | Glatten on schedule/on budget, +50% capacity; Spartanburg still unproven |\n| Mix rationality | 1 | Growth capex needed, but heavy buyback at cycle-peak multiple undermines discipline |\n| Guidance accuracy | 3 | FY26 raised twice; recent quarterly beats; 8/8 quality checks |\n| Insider bonus | \u22122 | Insider ownership 0.25%; no insider buying visible; class action/regulatory narratives |\n\n**Total: 6/15 + (\u22122) = 4/15 \u2014 Weak-to-Ad\u00e9quate.** Execution strong, capital allocation at current prices poor.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2.5 \u2014 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n### Step 2.5-1\u20132. R&D Capitalization and SBC Treatment\n- R&D expense not in pack, **not retrievable** \u2192 **[Unconfirmed]**; modelled at ~4.5% of revenue (~$160M FY25) [Assumption]. With amortisation ~1/3 of the stock, the EBIT uplift is ~$105M; the invested-capital uplift ~$320M.\n- Adjusted ROIC = (NOPAT ~$489M + $105M) / (IC ~$2,850M + $320M) \u2248 **18.7%** [Estimate].\n- SBC: $32M FY25, $33M FY24 [Actual \u2014 pack series]. Adjusted FCF = GAAP FCF \u2212 SBC = $377M TTM \u2212 $32M \u2248 **$345M TTM** [Estimate].\n\n### Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC vs GAAP\nGAAP ROIC ~17% \u2192 Adjusted ROIC ~19%. Gap <5pct: **no red flag**.\n\n### Step 2.5-6. Owner Earnings\nOwner Earnings = NI + D&A \u2212 Maintenance capex \u2212 \u0394WC = $552M TTM + ~$120M D&A \u2212 ~$140M (maintenance ~3.5% of sales) \u2212 ~$50M \u0394WC = **~$480M TTM** [Estimate].\nAt $20.3B market cap, the market pays **42x owner earnings**. This is the correct lens for a capital-intensive cyclical.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION (ENGINE 2: CYCLE-ADJUSTED)\n\n### Step 0 recap\n- **Archetype: C \u2014 Cyclical** (with Quality/Compounder features) \u2192 **Engine 2**\n- Transition watch: If ROIC > WACC for 3 consecutive cycles and margin volatility structurally compresses, reclassify to Quality Compounder; currently it is ~60/40 Cyclical/Quality, so a blended engine (Engine 2 \u00d7 60% + Engine 1 \u00d7 40%) is used.\n\n### Cycle Position\n- FY20 revenue \u221214% (COVID trough); FY21 \u221210%; FY22\u2013FY25: +6%, +22%, +14%, +7% [Actual \u2014 pack]\n- FY26 guide: +20\u201323% \u2014 well above trend; pricing contribution ~8% (normalizing to 3\u20135% FY27 per Zacks) [Actual/Estimate]\n- **Conclusion: Mid-to-late upcycle.** The 2020\u20132021 trough is the reference trough; the current revenue is ~31% above the prior 2019 peak in nominal terms.\n\n### Normalised Earnings\nTwo anchors:\n\n**Anchor A \u2014 Mid-cycle normalised (trough-to-average):**\n- Normalised revenue: ~$3.8B [Estimate \u2014 8-yr growth trend from $2.5B 2019 to $4.3B FY26E, blend]\n- Normalised EBIT margin: 17.0% (cycle-average, 2019\u20132026 margins range 7\u201319%)\n- Normalised NOPAT: $3.8B \u00d7 17.0% \u00d7 (1\u22120.225) \u2248 $500M\n- At 22x normalised P/E (aero supplier historical mid) \u2192 **$8.0B equity value \u2248 $130/share** [Estimate]\n\n**Anchor B \u2014 Forward DCF (explicit forecasts below):**\nThe market is paying for Anchor B with Anchor A safety. The DCF below shows what $343 implies.\n\n### Explicit DCF Forecast (FY26E\u2013FY30E)\n*All financial values in $M.*\n\n| Line | FY26E | FY27E | FY28E | FY29E | FY30E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Revenue** | **4,334** | **4,730** | **5,110** | **5,470** | **5,740** |\n| Growth % | +21.5% [guidance mid] | +9.1% [Assumption] | +8.0% [Assumption] | +7.0% [Assumption] | +5.0% [Assumption] |\n| EBIT margin | 17.8% | 18.2% | 18.5% | 19.0% | 18.5% |\n| **EBIT** | **771** | **861** | **945** | **1,039** | **1,062** |\n| Tax @ 22.5% | \u2212173 | \u2212194 | \u2212213 | \u2212234 | \u2212239 |\n| **NOPAT** | **598** | **667** | **733** | **805** | **823** |\n| + D&A | 140 | 155 | 170 | 185 | 190 |\n| \u2212 Capex | \u2212290 [guide] | \u2212240 [Assumption] | \u2212200 [Assumption] | \u2212200 [Assumption] | \u2212190 [Assumption] |\n| \u2212 \u0394WC | \u221280 [Assumption] | \u221250 | \u221240 | \u221230 | +30 |\n| \u2212 SBC | \u221240 | \u221245 | \u221250 | \u221255 | \u221255 |\n| **FCF** | **328** | **487** | **613** | **705** | **798** |\n| Check vs guide | $300\u2013350M \u2714 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n\n**Source map for every driver:**\n\n| Driver | Source |\n|---|---|\n| FY26E revenue | Company guidance +20\u201323% [Actual \u2014 Q3 FY26 release, Jul-29-2026] |\n| FY26E capex | Company guidance ~$290M [Actual \u2014 Q3 FY26 release] |\n| FY26E tax | Company guidance ~22.5% [Actual \u2014 Q3 FY26 release] |\n| FY26E FCF | Company guidance $300\u2013350M [Actual \u2014 Q3 FY26 release] |\n| FY27E revenue growth | My estimate from Zacks analysis: pricing normalises 3\u20135%, China on-highway exits (~$100M+), powergen customers raising FY27-28 forecasts [Estimate] |\n| FY28\u201330E growth | My assumption: aero OEM rates continue up, Spartanburg deliveries begin 2028, aftermarket grows; **the path beyond FY26 is NOT guided** [Assumption] |\n| FY27\u201328 Capex | Q4 FY25 transcript: \"elevated capex in fiscal 2026 and fiscal 2027\" [Actual]; I taper to $240M/$200M [Assumption] |\n| Margins | FY26 guided: Aero 23.5%, Industrial 19% [Actual]; I assume slow improvement as volume scales, then a cycle softness pullback [Assumption] |\n\n### WACC Derivation\n| Component | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rf | 4.70% | 10Y Treasury [Retrieved \u2014 YCharts, 2026-08-21] |\n| ERP | 5.0% | [Assumption] |\n| Beta | 1.15 | Vendor beta 0.88 understates cyclicality; using 1.15 forward-looking [Assumption] |\n| Cost of Equity | 4.70 + 1.15 \u00d7 5.0 = **10.45%** | \u2014 |\n| Cost of Debt (pre-tax) | 5.2% | [Assumption \u2014 BBB+ vs risk-free +50bp] |\n| After-tax CoD | 5.2 \u00d7 0.775 = **4.03%** | \u2014 |\n| Equity weight | 95.2% | Market cap $20.32B / EV ~$21.34B |\n| Debt weight | 4.8% | Total debt $1.342B [Actual \u2014 Q3 release] \u2212 cash $0.327B = net debt $1.015B |\n| **WACC** | **10.14% \u2248 10.1%** | \u2014 |\n\n### DCF Result\n| | FCF | PV @10.1% |\n|---|---|---|\n| FY26E | 328 | 298 |\n| FY27E | 487 | 402 |\n| FY28E | 613 | 459 |\n| FY29E | 705 | 480 |\n| FY30E | 798 | 493 |\n| Terminal | 798\u00d71.03/(0.101\u22120.03) = **$11,576** | PV = **7,156** |\n| **Enterprise Value** | | **$9,288** |\n| \u2212 Net debt | | \u2212 $1,015 |\n| **Equity value** | | **$8,273** |\n| / 61.5M shares | | **$134.5 / share** |\n\nThis \"cold DCF\" with conservative margins (but reasonable growth) yields only **$135**. Even a generous bull DCF at 12% growth and 20% terminal margins yields ~**$250\u2013280** (see reverse-DCF below).\n\n### Reverse DCF \u2014 What the Market Implies\nTo justify $343.26 / $20.3B equity:\n- Required FCF CAGR FY26\u2013FY35: **~11\u201312%**, with terminal FCF margin **15\u201316%** and **no cyclical downturn**, OR\n- FY30 revenue = $7.5\u20138.0B (CAGR ~13%), EBIT margin = 21%, FCF margin = 14%, terminal multiple = 25x.\n**Judgment: Feasible but at the top ~10% of the scenario distribution; inconsistent with base rates.** The consensus forward growth of 13.5% EPS is inside the feasible range but assumes zero cycle \u2014 the market is paying a compounder multiple for a cyclical.\n\n### 3-X. Tornado Sensitivity (Top-3 drivers \u2192 Thesis KPIs)\n1. **FY27\u201328 revenue growth** (\u00b13pts) \u2014 swing on IV: \u00b1$60\u201380/share\n2. **Terminal FCF margin / cycle timing** (\u00b1200bp) \u2014 swing: \u00b1$70/share\n3. **Sustained pricing (3\u20135% normalisation vs 0%)** \u2014 swing: \u00b1$40/share\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n1. **Competitive response:** If WWD's high-speed fuel-injection and grid/ICE controls become visibly hot, Liebherr, Collins, Moog, and Chinese local players will add capacity/funding; MOG.A and RTX are direct peer references. Competitive response risk: **\u22121 (mild)**.\n2. **Reflexivity \u2014 negative loop:** The ~24% drawdown from $450 to $343 (despite beats) signals investor fear of an FCF trough; if the share stays below sell-side targets, SBC/talent retention stays fine (small SBC), but M&A currency weakens and customer confidence is only marginally affected \u2014 **net neutral** for this company.\n3. **Customer behaviour evolution:** Aftermarket content increases with fleet age; early adopters of new engine programmes (LEAP) convert to high-margin services as instalments grow. The mix shift toward services is already visible: commercial services +24% vs commercial OEM +34% in Q3 FY26. LTV/CAC improving.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIO ENGINE\n\n### Step 4-1..2. Scenario Composition\n| Scenario | Probability | Logic |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Bear** | 25% | Aero rate recovery stalls; industrial demand rollover; China exit drag; tariffs resume; pricing falls to 0%; FY28 EPS $8.20; multiple 18x; price $148 |\n| **Base** | 50% | Growth normalises: FY27 +9%, FY28 +8%; margins 18.5%; FY28 GAAP EPS ~$10.50; multiple 25x; price $262 |\n| **Bull** | 25% | Aero supercycle + grid/data-centre boom; 12% growth sustained; margins 20% by FY29; FY28 EPS $12.20; multiple 28x; price $342 |\n\n### Step 4-3. Bayesian Update\n- Prior: 35% odds of \"cyclical rollover within 3 years\" (base rate).\n- Likelihood from FY26 evidence: strong beats, raised guidance, order backlogs, but FCF dec